</p/><p><strong>Summary: </strong>
Musing on the finite nature of a CD and the false infinity of streaming.
</p>
        <p>A few months ago, we stopped in at a pop-up shop that had lots of music
equipment. A musician was downsizing and had some fantastic stereo equipment for
sale. We purchased it and <time datetime="2026-06-23" title="2026-06-23">two days ago</time> I set up the stereo in the basement
recently cleared and cleaned by Jenny and her friend.</p>
<p>I dug through our <span>Compact Discs</span> (<abbr title="Compact Discs">CDs</abbr> <small><a class="ref" rel="tag opener" aria-label="Other site-wide references of “Compact Discs”" title="Other site-wide references of “Compact Discs”" href="https://takeonrules.com/site-map/glossary/#abbr-dfn-GLOSSARY-COMPACT-DISC">&#128214;</a></small>)
 and pulled out a cardboard album holder containing U2&rsquo;s
<cite>Achtung Baby</cite>; the first <abbr title="Compact Disc">CD</abbr>
 that I owned.  A fitting test for this
newly assembled system.</p>
<p>I inserted the <abbr title="Compact Disc">CD</abbr>
, pressed play, and dialed the volume about a third of the way
up and listened as the distortion of <cite>Zoo Station</cite>burst from the
speakers.  In that moment I felt as though I were communing with my past self;
present both as a teenager and a gray haired middle-aged man.  Filling with a
memory of place—of hardwood floors and oak trim I helped strip that first summer
we moved to Goshen.</p>
<p>With the album now spinning, I sat in the recliner, I didn’t pull out any
devices as habits and reflexes of-late demand.  Instead I listened to the full
sound.</p>
<p>As I listened, I thought of Austin, for whom I dubbed a cassette tape of the
album.  The day after I gave it to him, he returned the tape saying something
was off in the recording.  I recorded again, then gave it back to him.</p>
<p>The problem: the first track, <cite>Zoo Station</cite>, with its distortion and
static, was such a departure from <em>Joshua Tree</em> that Austin thought there was
something wrong with the recording.</p>
<p>I also thought of Matt, who lamented that in the first year of <cite>Achtung Baby</cite>that his friends played it incessantly.  I played it as it was my only
<abbr title="Compact Disc">CD</abbr>
.  Jeremy played it to help cope with a recent break-up.</p>
<p>I no longer interact with Matt nor Jeremy, as that season of life has now drawn
fully to a close.  Austin and I had already drifted apart in high school, and
I’ve only seen him once in the last decade; for our 20th high school reunion.</p>
<p><time datetime="2026-06-24" title="2026-06-24">Yesterday</time>, I pulled out INXS&rsquo;s <cite>Welcome to Wherever You Are</cite>(my
second <abbr title="Compact Disc">CD</abbr>
).  I don’t remember listening to it in our living room.  But instead I
recall listening to it on a portable <abbr title="Compact Disc">CD</abbr>
 player.  Yet I have no memory of the
space in which I listened.  It was as though that portable and personal player
stripped me of my anchoring in the physical world.  Perhaps that—had I been
attuned—would have been a harbinger of things to come.  One that portended a
loss of connection to location.</p>
<p>Listening to a single <abbr title="Compact Disc">CD</abbr>
 with my body several feet away from the player, I found
myself sticking with the album as constructed.  Were I listening on a portable
device with either device-local files and/or streaming services, I might choose
to skip a song, or jump to another album entirely.</p>
<p>I’m not an audiophile, but the system with its analogue dials, produced a warm,
broad, and expansive sound.  With music filling the room, instead of just my
ears, I felt as though I were connecting just a bit more with the artists and
producers that composed the album.  Listening to something intentionally crafted
into a complete experience.</p>
<p>Unfortunately I no longer own the physical media for my third album: R.E.M.’s
<cite>Automatic for the People</cite>.<small class="side-container">
  <span class="side-label"><span class="hidden">(</span>Sidenote<span class="hidden">:</span></span>
  <span class="side" role="note"> After writing this paragraph, but before publishing, I did in fact find my
original copy of <cite>Automatic for the People</cite>.  I spun it up
immediately.<span class="hidden">)</span></span>
</small>
 One that I purchased in Chicago during
a class trip for my junior year of high school.  Several years ago, we had a
garage sale, and I opted to sell off many of my <abbr title="Compact Discs">CDs</abbr>
; rationalizing that they
took up space and I could <em>always</em> stream them.</p>
<p>Yet in shifting from one medium to another, I lost a bit of something—cohesion.</p>
<p>Yes, I could stream and listen to an album in it’s entirety, but in that
streaming medium, an album is not something discrete but instead an arbitrary
slice of the gluttonous infinite; one in which the rent-seeking vampiric systems
attempt to project the eternal and inevitable.</p>
<p>I find myself thinking about this newly cleared basement, a kind of listening
and observation room.  A place where the stereo and the physical media players
are a key feature.  I’m wondering how this activity of placing a <abbr title="Compact Disc">CD</abbr>
 into the
player brings about a different experience than sifting through a Borgesian
infinity?</p>
<p>I contemplate finitude: a reminder that seasons pass, as does everything, just
in its own time and way.  I think of the times I had streamed and listened to
<cite>Achtung Baby</cite>; how it failed to bring about that state of communion.
Yet listening to the <abbr title="Compact Disc">CD</abbr>
 on a stereo invoked those memories.</p>
<p>And I’m left wondering.</p>
<p>What are some albums, were you to listen to completion, might invoke a memory of
time and place?  How old is that memory?</p>

